## Why This Is Asked

In multi-tenant LLM-ops, data lineage and governance are critical for regulatory compliance, debugging, and customer trust. This question probes design of a tamper-evident provenance system, tenant isolation, and integration with existing catalogs.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and model-versioning
- Tenant isolation and access controls
- Auditable, tamper-evident logs
- Retention policies and legal holds
- Catalog integration and metadata propagation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal provenance record sketch
type RunRecord = {
  tenantId: string;
  runId: string;
  inputHash: string;
  promptMeta: Record<string, any>;
  sources: string[];
  modelVersion: string;
  decodeParams: Record<string, any>;
  timestamp: string;
  retentionDays: number;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce per-tenant retention while enabling legal holds?
- How would you integrate with Snowflake's data catalog and Hugging Face datasets?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluate knowledge of cryptographic provenance, data locality, and auditable ML workflows in multi-tenant, cloud-agnostic environments. Tests ability to reason about key management, TEEs, and detection of data leakage under load.

## Key Concepts
- Verifiable provenance with per-tenant keys
- Attested inference in a trusted enclave
- Region-bound data locality and KMS
- Tamper-evident audit logs and rotation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: sign a payload with a per-tenant key (simplified)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signPayload(payload, key) {
  const sign = crypto.createSign('SHA256');
  sign.update(JSON.stringify(payload));
  sign.end();
  return sign.sign(key, 'base64');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate keys without downtime?
- How would you measure and ensure latency impact and leakage risk?

## Why This Is Asked
This measures practical ability to implement tenant-aware routing and observability in an LLM gateway, balancing simplicity with correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy mapping
- Latency measurement and logging
- Safe defaults and fallback
- Cache invalidation on policy updates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo routing logic
function routeRequest(tenantId, req) {
  const policy = getPolicy(tenantId) // {modelVersion, fallback}
  const version = policy?.modelVersion ?? 'v1';
  const backend = selectBackend(version);
  const t0 = performance.now();
  const res = backend.call(req);
  logRoute(tenantId, version, performance.now() - t0, res.success);
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test concurrent policy updates without impacting in-flight requests?
- How would you handle deprecation of an old model version without breaking tenants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world hybrid edge-cloud serving with data locality, SLA management, and per-tenant isolation under fluctuating network conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality and residency per tenant
- Edge vs cloud routing and burst handling
- Per-tenant model pools and versioning
- Observability, fault tolerance, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluator
function routeRequest(req, policyStore){
  const p = policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  if(p.locality === 'edge' && req.latencyBudget >= EDGE_LATENCY){
    return 'edge';
  }
  return 'cloud';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate locality guarantees under variable networks?
- How would you automate canary rollouts and safe rollback per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design SLA-aware routing with multi-tenant fairness, regulatory constraints, and cost.

## Key Concepts
- SLA/QoS, model pools, edge vs cloud, real-time routing, circuit breakers, fault isolation, observability.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for routing decision
function route(request, tenancy, pools, metrics) {
  const budget = tenancy.latencyBudget;
  const eta = estimateETA(request, pools, metrics);
  const chosen = (eta <= budget) ? selectPool(pools, tenancy) : escalateFallback(tenancy);
  return chosen;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test burst traffic and backpressure across tenants?
- How would you validate data locality guarantees under failover?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to implement low-latency, per-tenant policy gating with live hot-swaps, immutability guarantees, and rollback strategies in a production LLM-ops gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Per-brand policy isolation and versioning
- Immutable policy versions with blue/green rollout
- Fast policy evaluation (sub-20 ms) and robust auditing
- WASM/Rust prototype for safe, sandboxed evaluation

## Code Example

```rust
// Minimal WASM-friendly policy evaluator API
pub fn eval(input: &str, policy_json: &str) -> bool {
    // naive policy: blocklist example
    if policy_json.contains("blocked") {
        return !input.contains("blocked");
    }
    true
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor policy drift and trigger automatic rollbacks?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure low false negatives with high safety?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design safe, incremental releases with robust privacy controls and automated rollback mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- Canary deployments with tenant isolation
- Per-tenant performance and policy monitoring
- Privacy-safe telemetry and leakage detection
- Lightweight data models for deployment plans, monitoring logs, and policy validation

